1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,676
Coding agents are quick to say a bug is fixed.

2
00:00:03,726 --> 00:00:06,463
VeriFix is an agent that has to prove it.

3
00:00:06,513 --> 00:00:08,570
I'm Shivansh, from Team HyperX.

4
00:00:09,733 --> 00:00:11,928
Judges start on the Verify screen.

5
00:00:11,978 --> 00:00:19,323
It checks seven claims against GitHub, neatlogs and our own run records, and links the evidence behind each one.

6
00:00:20,172 --> 00:00:22,336
Now, a live run on AWS Lambda.

7
00:00:22,386 --> 00:00:22,926
The bug:

8
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:27,796
a ledger fee function that loses cents to floating-point rounding.

9
00:00:27,846 --> 00:00:34,364
Before editing anything, VeriFix uses Entire Graph to find every caller of that function.

10
00:00:34,414 --> 00:00:37,463
For this demo, I inject a bad first patch.

11
00:00:37,513 --> 00:00:41,595
It passes the bug's own test, but it breaks two callers.

12
00:00:43,496 --> 00:00:48,871
VeriFix runs the caller tests too, catches the regression, and rolls the workspace back.

13
00:00:48,921 --> 00:00:52,815
The second attempt fixes the bug and keeps every caller passing.

14
00:00:52,865 --> 00:00:55,959
Only then does it issue a verification certificate.

15
00:00:58,106 --> 00:01:00,515
Every run sends a trace to neatlogs:

16
00:01:00,565 --> 00:01:03,453
each model call, tool step, token and cost.

17
00:01:03,503 --> 00:01:06,390
We used those traces to test our own claim.

18
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:19,985
In a controlled experiment, with four bugs, five repeats, and the same model and prompt, Entire Graph context used sixty-two percent fewer tokens than reading the whole repository, and lost no fixes.

19
00:01:20,035 --> 00:01:25,928
Our earlier ninety-seven percent figure came from an unfair baseline, so we retired it.

20
00:01:27,078 --> 00:01:28,375
Entire does two jobs.

21
00:01:28,425 --> 00:01:32,865
At run time, Entire Graph tells the agent what its change could break.

22
00:01:32,915 --> 00:01:40,820
During development, Entire checkpoints recorded the agent sessions behind our commits, so you can see how VeriFix was built.

23
00:01:41,778 --> 00:01:48,199
Our business plan, in the cfo.ai planning format, starts from the token usage we measured in neatlogs.

24
00:01:48,249 --> 00:01:52,068
The twenty-four month projection breaks even in month eleven.

25
00:01:52,118 --> 00:01:57,334
A stress test asks what happens if every run costs as much as our earliest version:

26
00:01:57,384 --> 00:01:59,047
model costs exceed revenue.

27
00:01:59,097 --> 00:02:01,267
These are projections, not results.

28
00:02:02,217 --> 00:02:04,481
We also looked at a real codebase:

29
00:02:04,531 --> 00:02:05,639
the neatlogs SDK.

30
00:02:05,689 --> 00:02:15,306
We found telemetry being dropped under concurrent load, reported it as issue one ninety-nine, and opened pull request two oh three with a fix.

31
00:02:16,656 --> 00:02:17,122
VeriFix:

32
00:02:17,172 --> 00:02:19,899
a coding agent that has to prove its fixes.

33
00:02:19,949 --> 00:02:22,935
Every number you've seen links to its evidence.

34
00:02:22,985 --> 00:02:25,905
Open the Verify screen, and check it yourself.

